<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Milton Margai</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1895">1895</a>–<a href="/years/1964">1964</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Milton Margai was a physician and the first prime minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a>. A founder of the Sierra Leone People's Party in 1951, he led the colonial government under the constitutional reforms of the 1950s, negotiated independence and became prime minister when Sierra Leone became independent on 27 April 1961. He held office until his death in 1964.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Margai was born on 7 December 1895 in Gbangbatoke, Moyamba District, in the British protectorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a>, the eldest of eighteen children of Mende parents; his father was an affluent trader and his grandfather a Mende warrior chief. After the Albert Academy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freetown">Freetown</a> and a degree in history from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourah-bay-college">Fourah Bay College</a> in 1921, he qualified as a doctor at the Durham University College of Medicine in 1926 and also attended the Liverpool School of Tropical Medicine, becoming the first doctor from the protectorate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Returning in 1928, he served in 11 of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>'s 12 districts in the Colonial Medical Service and campaigned on hygiene and social welfare. Working with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sande-society">Sande</a>, the Mende women's society that handed down knowledge of midwifery, he set up camps teaching hygiene to its initiates, wrote a manual on midwifery in Mende and trained the midwives who became known as Mamma Nurses; he described the work in his 1948 article Welfare Work in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-society">Secret Society</a>. He entered the Protectorate assembly in the 1930s, and in 1951 he founded the Sierra Leone People's Party with Siaka Stevens, which won that year's election to the Legislative Council.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>After heading the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> of health, agriculture and forestry, Margai became chief minister. In 1958 his brother Albert narrowly won a party challenge to his leadership but declined it and founded the opposition <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-national-party">People's National Party</a>, rejoining him in coalition in 1960, the year Margai led the delegation to the constitutional talks in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> with the Colonial Secretary Iain Macleod. Pro-British and conservative, he sought good relations with the British government after independence. His party won the general election of 27 May 1962, and he appointed northern leaders to key ministries to ease the grievances of northern groups.</p><p>He concentrated on education, health and agriculture but was criticised for suppressing the opposition All People's Congress and sometimes withholding funds from councils it controlled. He gradually brought Sierra Leoneans into command of the army, whose officers were ethnically mixed by 1964. He died in office on 28 April 1964, aged 68, and was succeeded by Albert. The Milton Margai School for the Blind in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freetown">Freetown</a>, whose foundation stone he laid in 1962, and the Milton Margai College of Education and Technology carry his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Milton Margai” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Sierra Leone Colony and Protectorate became independent on 27 April 1961 under Prime Minister Sir <b>Milton Margai</b> of the Sierra Leone People's Party.</blockquote><a href="/state/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a></li><li><blockquote>Elections and constitutional reforms in the 1950s brought <b>Milton Margai</b> to power, and Sierra Leone became independent on 27 April 1961.</blockquote><a href="/state/sierra-leone-colony-and-protectorate">Sierra Leone Colony and Protectorate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freetown">Freetown</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=8.49,-13.24&amp;year=1939" aria-label="Freetown on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4184 1847 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Milton Margai"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4634 2117h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4634 2117h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=8.49,-13.24&amp;year=1939">What was here in 1939? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
